 "warnings": [],
 "source": "\"\"\"Two names, one list.\r\n\r\nThe single most common Python surprise. `b = a` does not copy anything -- both\r\nnames point at the same object, so mutating through one is visible through the\r\nother. Watch the arrows: two variables, one heap object.\r\n\r\nRebinding is different from mutating, and the picture shows why.\r\n\"\"\"\r\n\r\na = [1, 2, 3]\r\nb = a               # same object -- two arrows, one list\r\nb.append(4)         # mutates what BOTH names see\r\n\r\nc = a[:]            # a slice copies -- a second object appears\r\nc.append(5)         # only c changes\r\n\r\nd = a\r\nd = [9, 9]          # rebinding d, not mutating: a is untouched\r\n\r\nprint(\"a =\", a)\r\nprint(\"b =\", b)\r\nprint(\"c =\", c)\r\nprint(\"d =\", d)\r\nprint(\"a is b:\", a is b, \" a is c:\", a is c)\r\n",
